x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$" 13194139535103 true] [17592186331318 1113 #uuid "8fbae037-c4bb-5f2f-80b6-5df3a6fe74f8" 13194139535103 true] [17592186331318 1145 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/62925844de27c9731b181f17a1bdd912bfa3265176bd62ebda425d182ae8d9e8.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not completely cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535103 true] [17592186331319 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331320 13194139535103 true] [17592186331319 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331321 13194139535103 true] [17592186331319 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331322 13194139535103 true] [17592186331319 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331323 13194139535103 true] [17592186331319 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331324 13194139535103 true] [17592186331319 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535103 true] [17592186331319 1041 "selection" 13194139535103 true] [17592186331319 1104 #edb/ref 17592186331323 13194139535103 true] [17592186331319 1110 #uuid "0bfd5d1e-89a7-5769-a08d-81d7dea2c416" 13194139535103 true] [17592186331320 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535103 true] [17592186331320 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535103 true] [17592186331320 1111 #uuid "0e3b2b2a-54a5-5cb4-b3b1-c64f7064db44" 13194139535103 